# Cloudflare Pages redirects — Al-Madinah International College.
#
# The WEC-LC redirect table that used to live here is archived at
# archive/wec-lc/_redirects. Not merged: all 74 of its generated routes point
# at pages (/academics/, /governance/, /students/, /press/, /faq/) that are no
# longer served, and a redirect to a 404 is worse than a 404 — it looks like
# the site meant to send you somewhere.

# ── APEX TO WWW ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every canonical URL the College publishes — in <link rel="canonical">, in
# sitemap.xml, in hreflang, on every printed instrument — is on the www host.
# If the apex also serves the site, the same page exists at two addresses,
# which splits how search engines see it and makes any origin-scoped setting
# ambiguous.
#
# Inert until al-madinahcollege.com (the apex) is attached to this Pages
# project as a custom domain. It costs nothing meanwhile and starts working
# the moment the domain is added; nothing here can affect the www host.
https://al-madinahcollege.com/*  https://www.al-madinahcollege.com/:splat  301!

# ── THE MADINAH SUBFOLDER ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The College was built into /madinah/ while the domain still served WEC-LC
# at the root. Those URLs were shared for review, and the review artefact
# links them, so every one of them keeps working — permanently, not for a
# grace period. The `ar` rule is first because /madinah/ar/x/ must become
# /ar/x/ and not /ar/ar/x/.
/madinah/ar/*  /ar/:splat  301
/madinah/*     /:splat     301

# ── HTML EXTENSIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The build writes directory-style paths (/about/, not /about.html). A
# visitor arriving from an old link, a printed document or a mistyped address
# should land on the page rather than a 404.
/index.html          /                    301
/about.html          /about/              301
/admissions.html     /admissions/         301
/awards.html         /awards/             301
/contact.html        /contact/            301
/faculties.html      /faculties/          301
/regulations.html    /regulations/        301
/standing.html       /standing/           301
/safeguarding.html   /safeguarding/       301
/calendar.html       /calendar/           301
/riwaq.html          /riwaq/              301

# ── SHORTHANDS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Short paths a person is likely to type, or to be given verbally by the
# Registry over a telephone. Each one resolves to a page that exists — the
# mistake the archived table made was keeping shorthands whose destinations
# had been retired.
/apply        /admissions/#apply           301
/charges      /admissions/charges/         301
/fees         /admissions/charges/         301
/tuition      /admissions/charges/         301
/programmes   /faculties/                  301
/quran        /faculties/quran/            301
/rules        /regulations/                301
/disclosure   /standing/                   301
/chains       /awards/register-of-chains/  301
/asanid       /awards/register-of-chains/  301
/ijazah       /awards/#ijazah              301

# /verify is NOT redirected, and must not be. It is a real page at /verify/,
# and a wildcard rule pointing /verify/* at it would drop whatever followed
# the slash — so a credential link someone had mistyped, or a QR code that
# had been re-encoded, would resolve to an EMPTY verification page rather
# than an error. An empty verification page is the one failure this route
# must never produce: it looks like a check that ran.
